WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.180
Kings of the Beach!

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:26.500
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.520
I'm Mike.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:28.880
I'm Sean.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.240
And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.700
And while I'm sure we'd all love to be playing some beach volleyball right now, it's the winter,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.880
and we actually just had our first snowfall in New York City today, which was like, they

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.700
wouldn't shut up about how it was a record that it like hasn't snowed in January in New

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:50.900
York City for like the first time in 155 years, which is really just shorthand for the first

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.580
time since it's been recorded, because they didn't record snowfall like that before 155

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:01.580
years ago. But we had to be respectful to, you know, our listeners down under because it's summer there.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.980
And that always throws me for a loop.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.820
How many, how often do you guys think about the fact that like when you're freezing your

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.320
ass off that it's actually summer for like half this world?

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:18.560
Yeah, it's really weird because like, like if I were to travel to Australia like a day before

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:25.480
my birthday, then I would have my birthday delayed by like six months and then like backwards

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:32.140
as well. So if you just keep traveling, like from north to south, north to south, you'll never age,

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.200
at least on paper.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.880
That checks, that adds up.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.940
There's absolutely no flaws in that line of it.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.260
Well, I think it's just that the flight itself takes that same amount of time.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.160
So technically you're aging in the flight, right?

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.340
Like it takes like six months to get to Australia.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.100
Something like that. Well, they really are just flying you around and then they land you somewhere in South America

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.160
and tell you something like that.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.920
That's true. I think they're not about that.

