00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.300
And then I was like, okay, yeah, this is like a decent challenge.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:18.820
And, you know, I, I thought it functioned as well as I could ask it to.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:23.500
And in my opinion, as far as like just the, the basic controls of volleyball.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:36.280
Yeah. I think, um, uh, the, the one nitpick that I had about the controls of this game is that it, sometimes the, the game would sort of like lock you into place.

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:39.980
Like if you ended up below the ball, it would lock you there.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:48.880
And that's probably for the best because, you know, depth perception has always kind of been a problem of ours in these, uh, net based games.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:56.880
Um, but there, there'd be times where I'm trying to sort of, uh, undercut the ball.

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:07.840
So, and like sort of jump under it and do like a direct spike back instead of like the standard bump set spike that would give the opponent more time to get into position.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.960
And sometimes it just, it just wouldn't let you do something like that.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:22.700
So sometimes it felt like you couldn't really ex not express yourself, but, uh, you couldn't really execute exactly what you wanted to execute.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.740
It's sort of like, no, you gotta do it this way.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:32.040
But other than that, like, I think it, it's a pretty good, um, simulation of the sport.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:37.500
Yeah. I did forget about that actually, but I had that issue a couple of times too, or it's locking me into place.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:42.580
And I, I felt a few times like it locked me into a place where it was actually not ideal.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:47.820
And I, and I missed because of it, but realistically I probably missed and then was blaming it on that.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:49.100
But it was, it's hard to tell.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:59.380
Sean, that's a great point because outside of the movement being a little on rails, especially as you get closer to the net, it, it sets you up for a spike and stuff like that.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:13.380
Uh, outside of that, the game controls exactly as I expect it to, um, in a good way, you know, uh, there's only so many things you're, you're, you can do, you know, you only have the A and the B button or pressing the A and the B button together.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:20.000
Those are your like three options for what you can do, uh, to, um, to hit the ball.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:25.020
And then the rest is all like directional inputs after hitting the ball and stuff like that.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:30.380
Or, um, you know, setting up for a defensive thing versus an offensive thing.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:46.920
Like the, the actual experience might get a little stale, like over time, but as somebody who was just playing this recreationally for this week's episode, I thought it was a very, like, not only competent, but it just surprised me how well it functioned as a volleyball game.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:15:00.860
Yeah. Like I kind of fell into a hole when I got, um, stuck in a blizzard earlier this, or late last year, um, of just playing Mario tennis on the N64.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:05.500
Uh, and it's, it's kind of just as simple.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:16.640
Like there are only so many inputs and the rest of it is sort of like after touch and like, there really isn't anything about like how long you hold down the button in this game.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:34.660
But it's, it's really just like two buttons and how long, how long you hold down the button and the directions and that simplicity yet having that kind of control can really make a game that a simple game like this have its life extended.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:42.840
Yeah. I agree completely because it's, it, it is, it just functions like a little volleyball game.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:58.320
You know, as weird as that sounds like, and I wonder if, you know, the simplicity of the controls, um, and, and just what was required to make it function, gave them more time to like put into the physics and stuff, which I also think feel pretty great.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:08.300
Like the, the, I don't know if I, it's just easier to notice in a volleyball game because of how much the ball is like being popped into the air and, and, and going up and down and stuff.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:13.820
But like compared to other sports games, I really noticed like, oh, that like moves like a volleyball feels like it would move.

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.740
I guess. There's lots of parabolas.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:24.380
Yeah. I mean, but it's like, but like even the way it just like, it's going faster when you first hit it and it slows down at the top and it speeds up.

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:30.660
Like, I don't know. It sounds like I'm like really like looking at this with closely, but there's so many NES games.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:36.160
I mean, I'm thinking particularly of earlier stuff in the, in, you know, like the mid eighties and stuff that we were working with.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:41.740
Like we're like the physics of any projectile feels clunky and it feels smooth.

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:49.340
Yeah. I think what, what else kind of just makes the game feel better is also because the game looks really good.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:58.140
Like these sprites, uh, are at once clearly defined and also look pretty realistic.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:07.340
And, uh, the animation, like it's a very smooth arc whenever you hit the ball and the ball itself even has some texture to it.

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:12.140
And there's some movement in the background, but the, but it's never distracting.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:15.720
Interesting. And you just got a lot of detail and crispness.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:20.940
I think that the, the art in this game is, is very good and expressive.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:26.840
If you don't know the rules of volleyball, you can only score if you, uh, if you're the one serving.

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:33.100
Otherwise, uh, you know, if you correctly like should have scored that point, but you weren't the one that serves.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:38.120
Now you get to serve and thus, um, the game can take a little longer than you might expect.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:40.860
It's 15 points, uh, to win the match.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:49.780
And if you're not using like the after touch feature of the direction pad at the right time to kind of change where you're, the ball is landing.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:56.340
I found because I wasn't doing that at first, the computer almost will never, uh, mess up that kind of stuff.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:03.420
It almost becomes, uh, like a juggling game where it's just four people having a nice volley, uh, with the ball back and forth.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:10.500
You have to do some kind of touching in, in my experience of the game, at least there's some kind of touching with the deep head to manipulate the ball a little bit,

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:18.500
or maybe be more precise about when you, uh, spike the ball to get it to hit a little harder to then, uh, hit their faces.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:24.220
There's a lot of, um, expression and, and character in this game though, too.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.420
And one of the things that really surprised me was the ability to argue calls with the referee.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:34.800
I think that that's, uh, an incredibly like future forward thinking thing.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:36.000
It's fun.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:43.260
It's silly. And it makes it okay that like the call wasn't correct because you have the chance to like do something about it.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:51.420
And I know we've talked in the past about that with like NFL games and being able to challenge a play and like, why should that be a feature in these kinds of games?

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:58.960
But when done appropriately and, and fitting the theme, uh, or, or sorry, the vibe of the game is a very like relaxing game.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:03.880
And it's fun to go up there and argue with the ref and he'll even give you a yellow card and then a red card.

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:07.140
If you argue too much, like it's, it's a nice touch.

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.760
I want you kick this, you kick sand at him.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.920
Yeah. I don't quite makes the wrong call.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:16.640
Remember how you argue with the ref?

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:21.160
My like computer teammate usually did that for me, but it was always funny.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:34.620
Uh, and in that same vein, like if you were to like pull off a pretty sweet point get, um, then you and your teammate will just randomly like high five and butt pat or whatever.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:38.880
Yeah. Um, I, I always find it interesting though.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:46.140
I'm curious, like I, I am of two minds about the whole arguing the call thing because like that is,

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:47.760
that does help immersion.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:52.360
And that is what happens in, in real volleyball or in any real sport.

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:57.540
But it's like, that's an imperfection in the sport because we are not computers.

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.340
I can't know for sure.

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:05.160
Like if a ball was out or something, but it's almost like we have the opportunity to fix that in perfection in the sport.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.880
So like, I can see it both ways.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:14.680
I can kind of see it being like, okay, it's cool for immersion, but also like, Hey, I want to know like who really won that.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:15.900
Was that in or was that out?

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:18.720
Like, yeah, no, I, I hear you.

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:27.100
And I think the difference is, is that in, in these kinds of sports, because that actually does happen, it becomes like a part of the game, you know?

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:29.240
Uh, certainly, certainly in tennis too.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:34.080
Now, like they actually, you know, they have computer replays like nobody else, you know,

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:38.980
they actually are able to like determine exactly where the ball was hit on the court and zoom in.

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:48.140
And if even a little, uh, hair of the shadow, uh, touched the, uh, the white line, then it's still in, you know, like they, they can get so precise with it.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:52.160
But in this game, it's like, you know, you might miss a call.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:54.700
You might not, you know, you might not want to challenge a call or whatever.

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:56.360
And that's like the risk that you have to make.

00:20:56.360 --> 00:21:11.120
Like, so there is some kind of like, you know, management there, at least I'm not saying there's any strategy to it, but there's definitely, uh, you know, you have to, you have to be paying attention to the play at all times and make the judgment on whether that was in or out.

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:14.280
Yeah. I mean, it totally, yeah, it totally makes sense for immersion.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:19.820
And just because it has become part of the game, but like I said, I'm just like, I can't fully back either side of it.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.120
Like I haven't decided what like I would actually prefer.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:32.300
I think, I think I would prefer it to just be perfect for, you know, just for the sake of like, it is a, it is a video game.

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:37.380
It knows whether it's actually in or out and then it chooses to add the randomness.

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:38.560
So that is kind of upsetting.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:44.600
Whereas in like, yeah, in the real world, people just make honest mistakes and that's why these things happen.

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:46.040
And that's why we added challenges.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:54.820
Hopefully one day we'll just be able to have robot, um, referees just tell us, you know, the actual results of everything.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:22:02.220
And we don't have to wait, uh, to find out after the fact or through video replay that, um, oh, actually turns over my, turns out my team was screwed.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:09.900
Yeah. Cause I'm thinking like, if I, if I'm playing this in tournament mode, I think it is kind of fun to like make the call.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:15.380
Cause I'm like immersed if I'm playing by myself in tournament mode, but I'm imagining also if I'm, you know,

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:26.680
if it's back in the nineties and I'm playing, you know, I'm playing against my older brother and, and I swear that like, I just won the game, but there was a bad call.

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:32.180
And now he's like, well, I mean, you know, the ref said, and I'm like, you know what?

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:39.160
Like I wish that the game would just tell me at that point, like if I'm playing against another human, I just want to know like who won.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:42.260
So there's no, so I can, uh, there's no disputes.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:48.480
Maybe you should just, uh, be more choosy as to when you argue with the ref, you know?

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.220
Right. That, that will have to be what it is.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.800
I often wonder how.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:56.560
Cause you do have recourse with like the ref, like you can argue it.

00:22:56.560 --> 00:23:04.660
I'm pretty sure it, if it did a wrong call on purpose, if you then argue with the ref, you should be able to get it.

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:05.740
Right. Yeah.

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.520
I'm assuming. Yeah.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:11.180
Okay. Yeah. It will never actually just like stick it to you and be like, no.

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:18.880
Yeah. I guess I'm just more okay with it because, you know, I come from a place where my favorite, in my favorite sport, we have trouble defining what a catch is.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:22.880
So you kind of, you kind of have to take that as it is.

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:28.880
Yeah. I do often wonder Sean, how American football was ever played before you could like review the tape.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:31.240
Like it must've been a different game.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:34.080
No, it was just like, you know, the ref was there.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.120
He's probably, he's probably got it.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.900
Or you thought that everything was rigged.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:41.760
Right. Which it is, but not in any particular way.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:47.740
It's kind of like how, you know, certain things are eyeballed for the most part until things get like really serious.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.500
Yeah. You know, a lot of people just get like, yeah, yeah.

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:51.760
He was like, that was a first down.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.580
Like, you know, and then certain times it's like, all right, this is important play.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:55.780
Like break out the chains.

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:56.880
We got to measure it.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:59.740
Like, you know, it's like other times they just give them away.

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.920
But then it's like, no, it's also funny with the chains.

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:04.820
Sorry to get so off topic.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:12.980
It's because like the initial spot, like where they put the ball to then measure it with the change is an imprecise thing.

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:14.400
Like there's like, oh, it was here.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:20.080
I think it's like, but then we have to measure it very precisely after I eyeballed it.

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:22.200
Anyway, I'm we're very off topic.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:27.440
Yeah. No, for true chain measuring or any kind of tape measuring in sports, see bocce.

00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:32.320
That is where, you know, that's where things are legit because those balls have been untouched.

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:33.560
They are where they are.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:38.700
And then you break out the measurement and make sure that you determine who's actually the closest to the white ball.

